WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.268
Welcome to Lecture 11 of the

2
00:00:02.339 --> 00:00:04.940
FrontierCS 2 course, on cryptography.

3
00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.610
This lecture comes in three parts.

4
00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.326
This lecture covers three tasks

5
00:00:10.326 --> 00:00:12.027
a hard secret to find

6
00:00:12.027 --> 00:00:14.394
a noisy computation to predict

7
00:00:14.394 --> 00:00:16.760
and a private lookup to shrink.

8
00:00:17.110 --> 00:00:20.950
We start with the secret: Structured LWE.

9
00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:23.641
Learning with errors, or LWE

10
00:00:23.714 --> 00:00:26.044
is the hard problem beneath much

11
00:00:26.117 --> 00:00:28.010
post-quantum cryptography.

12
00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.850
You are given a public matrix A

13
00:00:30.928 --> 00:00:32.951
and a vector b that equals

14
00:00:33.029 --> 00:00:35.831
A times a secret s, plus an error e

15
00:00:35.831 --> 00:00:36.920
all modulo q.

16
00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.150
Both the secret and the error are small.

17
00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:41.631
Without the error

18
00:00:41.694 --> 00:00:43.329
Gaussian elimination would

19
00:00:43.391 --> 00:00:44.900
find the secret at once.

20
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.490
The error is what makes it hard.

21
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.247
But extra structure

22
00:00:49.247 --> 00:00:52.273
such as a secret with few nonzero entries

23
00:00:52.273 --> 00:00:53.680
can open shortcuts.

24
00:00:54.030 --> 00:00:56.262
A paper at CRYPTO 2024 studies

25
00:00:56.336 --> 00:00:58.270
these structured variants.

26
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.255
Here is a small instance of our own

27
00:01:01.255 --> 00:01:03.593
twelve unknowns, twenty equations

28
00:01:03.593 --> 00:01:05.330
and modulus ninety-seven.

29
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.423
The secret is ternary

30
00:01:07.423 --> 00:01:10.749
so every entry is minus one, zero or one

31
00:01:10.749 --> 00:01:13.680
and exactly two entries are nonzero.

32
00:01:14.030 --> 00:01:17.870
Each error is also minus one, zero or one.

33
00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:19.970
How do we check a guess?

34
00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.241
With a simple version of the

35
00:01:22.310 --> 00:01:24.918
task's public rules: the right length

36
00:01:24.918 --> 00:01:27.800
the right alphabet, exactly two nonzeros

37
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:29.310
and a small residual

38
00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:31.162
b minus A times the guess

39
00:01:31.162 --> 00:01:34.250
centered modulo q, must be between minus one

40
00:01:34.319 --> 00:01:35.760
and one in every row.

41
00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.187
The all-zero guess fails

42
00:01:38.187 --> 00:01:40.430
the planted secret passes.

43
00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:43.066
And any secret that passes counts

44
00:01:43.066 --> 00:01:45.420
not only the one that was planted.

45
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.600
Pause and predict.

46
00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:51.078
Twelve entries, two of them nonzero

47
00:01:51.078 --> 00:01:53.030
each plus or minus one.

48
00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:55.140
How many candidates are there?

49
00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.496
Sixty-six ways to choose the two positions

50
00:02:01.496 --> 00:02:03.453
times four choices of sign

51
00:02:03.453 --> 00:02:05.130
two hundred sixty-four.

52
00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:09.370
Our search tries all two hundred sixty-four

53
00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:12.530
and exactly one passes: the planted secret.

54
00:02:12.880 --> 00:02:15.667
A dense ternary secret of the same length would

55
00:02:15.727 --> 00:02:17.921
have three to the twelfth candidates

56
00:02:17.921 --> 00:02:19.937
five hundred thirty-one thousand

57
00:02:19.937 --> 00:02:21.360
four hundred forty-one.

58
00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.238
At realistic sizes

59
00:02:23.238 --> 00:02:25.152
neither search is possible

60
00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.997
but sparsity still shrinks

61
00:02:27.065 --> 00:02:28.500
the space enormously.

62
00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:31.532
With, say, two hundred fifty-six unknowns

63
00:02:31.597 --> 00:02:33.036
and fourteen nonzeros

64
00:02:33.102 --> 00:02:35.588
there are about two to the eighty-nine

65
00:02:35.653 --> 00:02:36.830
sparse candidates

66
00:02:36.896 --> 00:02:38.924
against two to the four hundred

67
00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:39.970
six dense ones.

68
00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.567
Real attacks combine lattice reduction

69
00:02:42.626 --> 00:02:44.400
with guessing the sparse part.

70
00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.540
Here there is no solver to restore.

71
00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:50.186
The agent writes its own attacks and

72
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.290
submits one secret per instance.

73
00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:55.363
There are two hundred public instances

74
00:02:55.433 --> 00:02:57.179
in ten families of matrix

75
00:02:57.249 --> 00:02:58.715
and secret structure

76
00:02:58.715 --> 00:03:01.368
dense or sparse matrices, and binary

77
00:03:01.368 --> 00:03:02.486
ternary, small

78
00:03:02.486 --> 00:03:04.720
sparse or unrestricted secrets.

79
00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.863
The score is the share of instances solved

80
00:03:07.863 --> 00:03:10.590
and a pass means solving all two hundred.

81
00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.610
No model passed.

82
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.728
GPT-6 Astra and GPT-6.1 Terra

83
00:03:15.823 --> 00:03:18.400
solved eighty-four percent.

84
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.022
Claude Opus 5.5 solved seventy-two percent

85
00:03:22.022 --> 00:03:24.990
one hundred forty-four of two hundred.

86
00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.764
It solved all twenty in six families

87
00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.581
including all eighty instances of the four

88
00:03:30.647 --> 00:03:32.940
families with unrestricted secrets.

89
00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:34.580
In the other four

90
00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.730
it solved only the first six.

91
00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.600
Those have binary or ternary secrets

92
00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.560
with two to eight nonzeros.

93
00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.477
Structure makes a family attackable

94
00:03:44.477 --> 00:03:45.190
not easy.

95
00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:48.397
By the benchmark's difficulty measure

96
00:03:48.397 --> 00:03:51.370
Structured LWE is its second-hardest task.

97
00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:53.320
Three things to take away.

98
00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:55.882
LWE hides a small secret

99
00:03:55.974 --> 00:03:57.910
behind a small error.

100
00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:00.980
A sparse secret shrinks the search.

101
00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.650
And here the bar is absolute

102
00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:05.970
all two hundred, or no pass.

103
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:07.292
As an exercise

104
00:04:07.292 --> 00:04:10.013
allow secrets with one to three nonzeros

105
00:04:10.078 --> 00:04:12.152
and see whether the search still

106
00:04:12.216 --> 00:04:14.160
finds only the planted secret.

107
00:04:14.510 --> 00:04:17.150
Next, CKKS Noise.
